#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484357} :activity/id #uuid "2aeb0302-967f-5ddb-8bf1-16281ba96227" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049288 :topic/id #uuid "ea0735e9-7712-5a1c-a626-25cab33f54cb" :topic/math-academy-id 6386} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484314 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317453 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fe6e1ef-2a02-59be-967b-360af6440b14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315213} {:answer/id #uuid "2493fd79-af96-5964-b4d5-79c55216b0d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315324} {:answer/id #uuid "acf5a8df-8734-5bf9-9bcc-688e3e570e08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316092} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1a6513-a24b-5275-9db3-0cd9e31e4d3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316104}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315213} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b3c4e4e-5751-5f8c-b537-c9c21b1bec65" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316808} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "113bb42a-cce4-59ec-8300-8cfaa0e4ff29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315221} {:answer/id #uuid "514af182-9b72-5c63-aec6-2e477be64e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315582} {:answer/id #uuid "764ddf91-8316-5d5f-ae60-fa33202df071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315785} {:answer/id #uuid "82fa34d1-254e-5284-8f46-340093da20bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{4\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315841}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315582} :answer-field/id #uuid "f855de14-5251-533c-9172-a67d92d9e753" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f7afbc4a-cfc0-549a-b6ce-77b93f2927bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-343907" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}}=\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have ${\\omega }_{2}=\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{2}=\\omega ,$ the $n=2$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=2$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))+x(-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″} & =-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{2}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{2}}]+{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\pi A=1 \\\\ 2\\pi B=0\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=-\\frac{1}{2\\pi } \\\\ B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=-\\frac{x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3730dcd9-f2ec-50da-be0d-14c755f56a78"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484349 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01560cae-7dae-59d2-abe1-84a1d7d6c454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483571} {:answer/id #uuid "4acd6ba8-bd4d-5211-b24b-b92da18d82bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483701} {:answer/id #uuid "88c5fee9-6d57-52b7-8a9a-fc2fb915b829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483848} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a5f1d7-c219-529e-8a3d-b15c3dd5908b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{n(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483962}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483571} :answer-field/id #uuid "1af9059e-73b7-5797-a59d-c6b481acd050" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "88bf7711-bb0b-51a9-9d4e-44874b161912" :question/math-academy-id "q-343878" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}+36y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the free term and all the sine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+36({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms to find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(36-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(\\frac{324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the cosine coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{n}(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}{9}=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c1f72d93-f5f8-5ed4-a7c5-2788d86bcace" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484356}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484354 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26abab5a-e0d3-5a63-b7b9-8c8b067c042b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397692} {:answer/id #uuid "1bc27d3d-2228-5745-9f75-6ad665b3203a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397693} {:answer/id #uuid "c26489de-65c7-593c-9bc1-121a32b98a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397694} {:answer/id #uuid "efa4633c-d9b6-5177-bd10-dd0e9bfa3eb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397695}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397695} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d3c5e72-c342-555b-a87c-e14e208988df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397691} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc221971-12de-52c7-8478-0068ddd7a170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{54\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397697} {:answer/id #uuid "fe05ab27-ffe9-519c-a245-1e3105109d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397698} {:answer/id #uuid "96ad6016-80ff-5081-b817-514fd1499c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397699} {:answer/id #uuid "0e910e33-e3b2-5731-a6d5-5a16e1e9ad14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{216\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397700}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397699} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fa9bba7-0c1d-559d-9dd6-0cd8ed6cec73" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d694cb78-fd17-5d72-bd5e-fb91875a20a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-343891" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $1$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{36{\\pi }^{2}}=6\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $1$-periodic function $(L=\\frac{1}{2})$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have ${\\omega }_{3}=6\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{3}=\\omega ,$ the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x))+x(-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)-36{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{3}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)-36{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}]+36{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-12\\pi A=0 \\\\ 12\\pi B=\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=0 \\\\ B=\\frac{1}{108\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d16e1bd2-5d3a-57fc-8ce5-5535cc365650" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484314}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484356 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317450 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "474ff160-2d86-5c69-8f85-60a047da5ab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315534} {:answer/id #uuid "708fd803-efd9-508d-a532-3adcfc75816c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315751} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c52c8f-3852-5f92-80be-6b94f3557303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316242} {:answer/id #uuid "ec8a1480-b239-5433-afdd-61831369aa0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316430}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315751} :answer-field/id #uuid "338e5d77-3b2a-5f0d-9914-332ea1ae1ee2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316666} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02955be2-58f0-51d6-9945-0544a0ee1d0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315130} {:answer/id #uuid "04fb5a85-7568-58b9-89ef-e9f8c617bb00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315142} {:answer/id #uuid "0af17db2-155c-57c1-915c-b2b4ebbb8456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315177} {:answer/id #uuid "c6644cb0-f07f-5b82-9b35-30e6d381997b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316237}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316237} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fcafd77-7898-5bd9-bcc7-456c9841599f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6d225572-fd74-5350-9e69-efe599d6dfa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-343883" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-8y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)-8(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))=8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n-8\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}-8)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)=8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and sine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-8\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=8 \\\\ {b}_{n}(-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}-8)=\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-1 \\\\ {b}_{n}=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e1c91699-1134-58dc-ab5b-ea077a7ce0b0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484354}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484357 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484267 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cf7e913-fe58-5754-9149-b09245f9b60a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+64)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483642} {:answer/id #uuid "53e18eaf-8bd1-5628-92d7-801bfaf1946a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+64)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483717} {:answer/id #uuid "6175ce1f-2050-5a57-bf7b-da70f7498dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+64)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483754} {:answer/id #uuid "b701f81d-8ac2-5b72-b41d-29bb579466f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+64)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483935}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483642} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe77b4de-90c9-5d84-bcb6-651adb4b6345" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b49a66f8-383d-5ed3-a1a9-b2f05fd8c09d" :question/math-academy-id "q-343873" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-16y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the free term and all the sine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})-16({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}(\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}+16)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the cosine coefficients, we obtain \n\n$$\n-{a}_{n}(\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}+16)=\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=-\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+64)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+64)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e69e4c87-91c4-5dd6-a2e7-92fb618add77" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484349}}] :activity/title "Solving ODEs Using Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483556}]}
